{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Why\nADR 0112 decisions 1+2, the Docker accumulator. At the 2026-09-02 outage `/var/lib/docker`\nwas 17G (overlay2 16G across 67 layer dirs) while `docker system df` claimed ~9.5 GB with\nZERO dangling images — ~**6.5 GB of orphaned overlay2 invisible to `docker prune`\nentirely**. Four prune rounds reclaimed ~1.06 GB against a 29 GB problem. A cap reading\nDocker's own accounting cannot see the bytes that caused the outage.\n\n## Design\n\n### One budget, three DIFFERENT enforcement strengths\n`infra/scripts/docker-storage-cap.sh` (new) is the single source of truth for a\n`/var/lib/docker` budget with an internal split (ADR 0112: \"S2 and S5 are one budget, not\ntwo\" — writable layers live INSIDE overlay2, so two independent caps are double-counted or\nmutually violable). Measured env-settable defaults (decision 6): 20 GiB total, 5 GiB of it\nthe BuildKit share (today's `BUILD_CACHE_KEEP=5GB`, promoted to one place instead of\nduplicated), leaving 15 GiB of image/layer share S5 sub-divides.\n\n1. **BuildKit share (5 GiB) — the daemon's OWN GC**, at the source, on its own schedule:\n   `builder.gc` in `/etc/docker/daemon.json`. `docker builder prune --keep-storage` applies\n   the same cap on demand; it is a BuildKit-only flag, used for THIS share only.\n2. **Image/layer share (15 GiB) — RETENTION, because Docker has no image-store ceiling.**\n   No flag caps the image store, so the share is held by the repo's existing rollback-safe\n   retention: `docker image prune -f` (dangling) each cycle, plus — only when the MEASURED\n   footprint exceeds the share — a bounded sweep of the oldest per-release `compose-mcp:<sha>`\n   tags beyond `DOCKER_IMAGE_RETAIN` (default 3), via `docker image rm` WITHOUT `-f`, so the\n   daemon refuses while any container references the image and `:prev` plus the serving\n   release survive by construction (autodeploy.sh `:554`). Retention is weaker than a hard\n   cap; the alarm is what makes the residual visible.\n3. **Orphaned overlay2 — NEITHER.** Docker does not know those bytes exist, so no cap and no\n   prune reaches them. ALARM-ONLY, and that is the point. Remediation is a daemon-level\n   reclaim: hand-deleting under `/var/lib/docker` desynchronises the daemon's layer metadata\n   from the tree, which is why the runbook bans it outright.\n\n### The daemon.json schema is version-dependent, so it is PROBED, not assumed\nEngine 25.0 / BuildKit 0.13 added `policy[].reservedSpace|maxUsedSpace|minFreeSpace` and\ndeprecated `defaultKeepStorage`/`keepStorage`. A wrong key is silently IGNORED — the cap\nwould simply not exist — so the script reads the engine version (`docker version --format\n'{{.Server.Version}}'`, else `dockerd --version` on first boot) and renders what THAT engine\nhonours: `maxUsedSpace` at >= 25.0, `defaultKeepStorage` below. Both are tested through a\nside-effect-free `--print-json --engine-version <v>` mode (the `--print-volumes` precedent).\nAn unreadable version renders the MODERN schema and logs it, and the merge is still\nvalidated — worst case \"no new cap plus a loud log\", never a broken daemon.\n\n### Install point, and how a LIVE daemon picks it up\nThe merge goes into `compose-up.sh` **ahead of `systemctl enable --now docker`** (today's\nfirst side-effecting step, line 55), so a first boot starts the daemon with the cap in place.\nOn a re-run against a LIVE daemon the write alone is inert, so the existing `daemon.json` is\nBACKED UP and the merged file VALIDATED (`dockerd --validate --config-file`, else a JSON\nparse); **a failed validation restores the backup and changes nothing** — a malformed\n`daemon.json` stops `dockerd` starting, a self-inflicted outage on the host being protected.\nOnly a validated CHANGE attempts `systemctl reload docker` (SIGHUP); if that fails the script\nWARNs naming the runbook step and **does not restart Docker**, because a restart takes Gerrit,\nthe review-bot and MCP down — an operator decision, not a boot-script side effect.\n\n### Detecting the bytes `docker system df` cannot see\nTwo INDEPENDENT measurements of the same bytes: filesystem truth — a bounded `du -sx` over\n`/var/lib/docker` and one over `.../overlay2` (`-x` does not cross into a mounted\n`overlay2/*/merged`; one `du` run counts a file hardlinked across layers once) — and Docker's\nledger, `docker system df` images + containers + build cache, sizes parsed to bytes.\n\n`docker_unaccounted_bytes` = overlay2 truth − ledger, clamped at 0. Some divergence is\nNORMAL: `du` counts allocated blocks including per-layer directory and whiteout overhead,\nwhile the ledger reports layer sizes with sharing accounted differently. So the threshold is\nnot \"any divergence\": 2 GiB, far above that overhead on a 16 GiB tree and far below the\n6.5 GB that went unnoticed — a terraform literal operators retune.\n\n## Metrics (`rebar/host`, dimensionless, 5-min probe)\n`docker_storage_bytes`, `docker_storage_used_percent`, `docker_buildkit_cache_bytes`,\n`docker_buildkit_cache_used_percent`, `docker_unaccounted_bytes` — published ONLY on a\nsuccessful measurement, so a failed probe publishes nothing and `treat_missing_data =\n\"breaching\"` pages instead of reading as a healthy 0 (observability.sh 2e's rule).\n\n## Alarms — three, none implying another\n1. `rebar-docker-storage-cap-high` — the whole budget saturating.\n2. `rebar-docker-buildkit-cache-high` — the BuildKit GENERATOR at its own cap. BuildKit at\n   100% of its 5 GiB share is invisible inside a 20 GiB budget reading 60%; a runaway image\n   set saturates the budget while BuildKit sits at 10%. The 3e92 argument: alarm on the\n   generator, not only on aggregate fullness.\n3. `rebar-docker-unaccounted-overlay2` — orphaned bytes above 2 GiB. 1 and 2 say \"how full\";\n   only this says \"full of what prune cannot touch\", changing remediation from \"prune harder\"\n   (the measured ~1.06 GB failure) to a daemon-level reclaim.\n\nAll three: 300/3/2, `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"`, `alarm_actions` + `ok_actions` on\n`aws_sns_topic.alerts`, pinned BY NAME in `test_alarm_actions_terraform.py`'s watched set.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `docker-storage-cap.sh` holds the `/var/lib/docker` budget plus its BuildKit/image\n      split in ONE place, measured env-settable defaults; no cap constant is duplicated.\n- [ ] The rendered `builder.gc` matches the ENGINE VERSION — `maxUsedSpace` at >= 25.0,\n      `defaultKeepStorage` below — both tested.\n- [ ] `compose-up.sh` merges ahead of `systemctl enable --now docker`, backing up then\n      validating; a validation failure restores the backup, changing nothing.\n- [ ] A validated `daemon.json` change on a live daemon triggers `systemctl reload docker`,\n      never an automatic restart.\n- [ ] `autodeploy.sh` takes its BuildKit `--keep-storage` from the shared budget.\n- [ ] Over-share image retention sweeps only surplus per-release tags via `docker image rm`\n      without `-f`; `:prev` and the serving release survive.\n- [ ] Observability publishes the five metrics, `docker_unaccounted_bytes` being `du` truth\n      minus `docker system df`; a failed probe publishes nothing.\n- [ ] Terraform declares the three alarms, SNS-wired, breaching, pinned by name; `fmt -check`\n      plus `validate` pass.\n- [ ] The runbook documents per-generator diagnosis/remediation, the operator restart step\n      for a changed `daemon.json`, plus a ban on deletion under `/var/lib/docker`.\n- [ ] Portability: host config plus local script tests; no CI-provider-specific trigger.\n\n## Testing\nRED-first: each test is written and observed FAILING before implementation. pytest runs are\n`env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" pytest -q <file>`.\n- `tests/scripts/test_docker_storage_cap.py` — budget/split derivation, per-engine-version\n  `builder.gc` rendering, idempotent merge preserving pre-existing keys, backup-restore on a\n  rejected config.\n- `tests/scripts/test_observability_docker_storage.py` — metric emission over stubbed\n  `docker`/`du`, size parsing, the unaccounted delta reproducing the incident's 16G-vs-9.5G\n  shape, silence (not 0) on probe failure.\n- `tests/unit/test_autodeploy_prune.py` — the budget drives `--keep-storage`; the sweep\n  spares `:prev` and the serving release.\n- `tests/unit/test_alarm_actions_terraform.py` — three alarms present, actioned, breaching.\n- `terraform fmt -check -recursive infra/terraform`, then `init -backend=false` + `validate`.\n- `make lint`, `make typecheck`, `test_mechanism_delta.py`,\n  `test_predates_gate_guard_parity.py`, `test_api_surface_gate.py`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788531793023115001,"uuid":"33b81ea1-605f-4a6d-aa7e-f78e5e6c0d78"}